  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(0-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{49}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+3)^{2}+(49\\cdot 16) & =9\\cdot 16 \\\\ 9(x+3)^{2}+784 & =144 \\\\ 9(x+3)^{2} & =-640 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-\\frac{640}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+3)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that $(x+3)^{2}=-\\frac{640}{9}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point.",
